58 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186254246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256358 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f893dcb4-30ee-50df-9369-932bd543c3cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256608 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256608 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256608 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256608 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256608 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f3eb3e3-8dee-5051-9c40-8fe59ac84fa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256608 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/b2/b23ef6e4df05874a715271c578dfa57d5ddacabef2d0801edc4e736c7de66e17.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256613 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bd8ece4-afaf-5640-9ad2-c2e8ef66ae37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256613 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/98/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256616 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9efcc68b-3929-5bf9-ab56-4c19b9a57740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256616 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/6d/6ddbfef68a0b41a6ee3f11c46ff7821f7ff0dc23a16b4244b5e7a1f6579d7dd2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={2}^{2-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2-1}+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={2}^{3-x}+12 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({2}^{1-x}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256623 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256623 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256623 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256623 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256623 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec791f70-e6b1-5d28-9a5c-0e20d98d3307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256623 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/14/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186256144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256625 #edb/ref 1069 "q-122352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256625 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256625 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=1\\,m$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ac389cb5-50b0-5d9d-9387-c7be6e7524a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186256625 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/f0/f08c9d1e8dce3c0ae7c37719cadc3fc60bf5de3030ce8c44ca7528a98f3abf7e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8{e}^{1-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8{e}^{0}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16{e}^{1-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4{e}^{(1-x)/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{(1-x)/2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=1$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{(x-1)/2}=2t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
